A||||3||Brandon86SE||Removing a sending unit plug||||5||
Z||000000||Brandon86SE||11-03-2001||01:51 AM||||So I've put off getting my rally gauges working for a while, but today was the day they were supposed to go in, and be done!  I got the oil pressure (mechanical) working, and it is great!  That took me and my friend hardly any time at all.  Then we went after the water temp gauge (mechanical).  Well, for those that don't know, there is a spot in the head on the cabin side of the engine, and there is a plug in it.  Problem being that instead of using a bolt, or something smart, Pontiac has it plugged and the end is a square hole JUST under 3/8"!  We tried and tried and tried with allen wrenches, etc. and it wouldn't budge.  Then we bought a bit that had an end on it just under 3/8", and it fit the hole perfectly, but the plug would not budge!  The hole is sort of stripped now, but not too badly.  Has anyone managed to get this plug out, and if so, how?????  (preferably with leaving the engine in.....    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)  We had the decklid off, and the rad hose out of the way.  The little metal tube for the hose that is down there was quite in the way though, but we couldn't see an easy way to get that off.  Please give me some suggestions!<BR>Thanks,<P>Brandon<P>[edit]  added <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011822.html" TARGET=_blank>this thread</A> so you can see a pic of what I'm talking about.  The hole for the unit can be seen on the engine in this thread, it is just below and to the left of the valves.  (gorgeous engine BTW, whoever it belongs to)<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Field_Front_(sig)_1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Brandon86SE (edited 11-03-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Nashco||11-03-2001||04:28 AM||||Well, since you're using the right tool, but the plug is just stripping, it doesn't sound like a good situation.  I've never heard of a plug like that stripping out internally if you're using the right tool....that's pretty hard to do.  I'm not quite sure how I would attack this, I'd have to look at my engine because I can't wrap my brain around it this late at night <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>Here's a similar thread from the archives:<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20010924-2-009003.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20010924-2-009003.html</A> <P>Good luck!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||avengador1||11-03-2001||09:23 PM||||Did you try soaking it with kroil. It penetrates to 1 millionth of an inch. I took my whole engine apart without breaking or stripping a single bolt.I think I used a ratchet extension to remove that bolt with the square hole you're talking about. You can find kroil at <A HREF="http://www.kanolabs.com" TARGET=_blank>www.kanolabs.com</A> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||11-04-2001||02:47 AM||||Thanks for the compliment on the engine. That is a 3.4 that I will be putting in one of my '87 GT's tomorrow. Here is the way it looks right now....<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/engine4_2.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.whgraphics.com" TARGET=_blank>West Highland Graphics & Design</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
